from international_completion_helpers import add,save
add(1979,1,'Number Theory',5,'Rewrite the alternating harmonic sum as a consecutive tail, then pair denominators summing to 1979; primality ensures the numerator factor survives cancellation.',r'Positive integers $p,q$ satisfy $p/q=\sum_{j=1}^{1319}(-1)^{j+1}/j$. Prove $1979\mid p$.',r'''Subtracting twice the even-indexed terms from the full harmonic sum gives
$$\frac pq=\sum_{j=1}^{1319}\frac1j-\sum_{j=1}^{659}\frac1j=\sum_{j=660}^{1319}\frac1j.$$
Pair $j$ with $1979-j$. Each pair equals $1979/[j(1979-j)]$. The integer 1979 is prime (trial division by primes up to $\sqrt{1979}<45$ suffices), and every denominator factor is less than 1979. Thus the sum is $1979u/v$ for integers $u,v$ with $1979\nmid v$. From $pv=1979uq$, primality gives $1979\mid p$.''',['Modular arithmetic','Fractions','Primes','Pairing'])
add(1979,2,'Combinatorics',5.5,'A three-vertex majority in a pentagon always contains adjacent vertices; applying this twice forces each face monochromatic and then forces their colours to agree.',r'Two pentagons have vertices $A_1,\ldots,A_5$ and $B_1,\ldots,B_5$. Colour their ten sides and every segment $A_iB_j$ red or green. If no triangle whose three sides are coloured is monochromatic, prove the ten pentagon sides share one colour.',r'''If two sides at $A_1$ had different colours, choose three $B$-vertices joined to $A_1$ in one colour, say red, and let $A_1A_2$ be red. All three joins from $A_2$ must be green. Among three vertices of a pentagon two are adjacent. Their connecting side would form a monochromatic triangle with either $A_1$ or $A_2$, regardless of its colour. Thus the $A$-pentagon is monochromatic; the same argument applies to $B$.

Suppose their colours differ, say the $A$ sides are red and the $B$ sides green. Each $A_i$ can have at most two green joins to $B$, because three would include adjacent $B$-vertices and give a green triangle. Hence each of $A_1,A_2$ has at least three red joins. Their two three-element neighbour sets intersect, producing a red triangle with side $A_1A_2$. This contradiction proves both pentagons have the same colour.''',['Colouring','Graph theory','Pigeonhole principle'])
add(1979,3,'Geometry',4.5,'Reflect the starting point in the perpendicular bisector of the centres; the resulting crossed radii and reflection-reversed angles give congruent triangles throughout the common rotation.',r'Two intersecting circles share $A$. Two points start at $A$ and traverse the respective circles in the same direction, each at constant speed, completing one revolution simultaneously. Prove there is a fixed point equidistant from the moving points at every instant.',r'''Let the centres be $O_1,O_2$, and reflect $A$ across the perpendicular bisector of $O_1O_2$ to obtain $P$. Denote this reflection by $S$. At any instant the two radius vectors have undergone the same rotation $R_\theta$. Since reflection reverses rotation, $SR_\theta=R_{-\theta}S$.

Write $u=A-O_1$, $v=A-O_2$, and let $L$ be the linear part of $S$. Then $P-O_2=Lu$ and $P-O_1=Lv$. Thus the squared distances from $P$ are $|R_\theta u-Lv|^2$ and $|R_\theta v-Lu|^2$. Their norm terms agree. Their dot-product terms agree too, since $LR_\theta$ is a reflection and therefore a symmetric linear map:
$$(R_\theta u)\cdot Lv=u\cdot(LR_\theta)v=(R_\theta v)\cdot Lu.$$
Consequently the two distances are equal for every $\theta$.''',['Reflection','Rotation','Coordinates','Congruence'])
add(1979,4,'Geometry',4.5,'For each fixed distance from P, move toward the perpendicular foot to minimise the denominator; Cauchy–Schwarz then gives the exact maximum and its equality locus.',r'Let $P$ lie in a plane and $Q$ lie outside it. Determine all points $R$ of the plane maximising $(QP+PR)/QR$.',r'''Let $X$ be the perpendicular foot of $Q$, and put $d=PX$, $h=QX$, $a=PQ=\sqrt{d^2+h^2}$. For fixed $x=PR$, the denominator is smallest when $R$ lies on ray $PX$ if $d>0$. Then $QR=\sqrt{(x-d)^2+h^2}$ and Cauchy–Schwarz gives
$$\frac{a+x}{QR}=\frac{(x-d)+h\,(a+d)/h}{\sqrt{(x-d)^2+h^2}}\le\sqrt{1+\frac{(a+d)^2}{h^2}}.$$
Equality holds exactly when $x-d=h^2/(a+d)=a-d$, hence $PR=a$. Thus if $P\ne X$ there is one maximiser: the point on ray $PX$ at distance $PQ$ from $P$. If $P=X$, the same calculation is independent of direction, and the maximisers form the circle in the plane centred at $P$ with radius $PQ$.''',['Geometric inequalities','Cauchy–Schwarz','Locus','Optimisation'])
add(1979,5,'Algebra',4,'A weighted sum of the three moment equations becomes a sum of nonnegative squares; its vanishing confines all nonzero coordinates to one index, with zero needing separate attention.',r'Find all real $a$ for which nonnegative reals $x_1,\ldots,x_5$ satisfy $\sum_{k=1}^5kx_k=a$, $\sum_{k=1}^5k^3x_k=a^2$, and $\sum_{k=1}^5k^5x_k=a^3$.',r'''Multiply the first equation by $a^2$, subtract twice $a$ times the second, and add the third. This gives
$$\sum_{k=1}^5k(k^2-a)^2x_k=0.$$
Every summand is nonnegative. If every $x_k=0$, then $a=0$, which is valid. Otherwise some $x_j>0$, forcing $a=j^2$. Conversely for $a=j^2$, choose $x_j=j$ and all other coordinates zero; all three equations hold. Therefore the full answer is $a\in\{0,1,4,9,16,25\}$.''',['Algebraic identities','Sum of squares','Equations'])
add(1979,6,'Combinatorics',5.5,'Introduce an auxiliary starting vertex to obtain a two-state recurrence, then eliminate it and solve the resulting second-order recurrence with the correct absorbing boundary.',r'A walk starts at vertex $A$ of a regular octagon and at each step moves to an adjacent vertex, stopping at its first visit to the opposite vertex $E$. If $a_n$ counts walks stopping after exactly $n$ steps, prove $a_{2n-1}=0$ and $a_{2n}=((2+\sqrt2)^{n-1}-(2-\sqrt2)^{n-1})/\sqrt2$.',r'''Parity of distance to $E$ changes at every step, so odd stopping times are impossible. Put $A_n=a_{2n}$ and let $B_n$ count walks stopping at $E$ after $2n$ steps when starting two edges from $E$. Looking at the first two moves gives, for $n\ge2$,
$$A_n=2A_{n-1}+2B_{n-1},\qquad B_n=A_{n-1}+2B_{n-1}.$$
Here paths reaching $E$ during those first two moves contribute only when $n=1$, and are excluded for $n\ge2$. Initially $A_1=0,B_1=1$, so $A_2=2$. Eliminating $B$ yields $A_n=4A_{n-1}-2A_{n-2}$ for $n\ge3$. The two characteristic roots are $2\pm\sqrt2$; the initial values give
$$A_n=\frac{(2+\sqrt2)^{n-1}-(2-\sqrt2)^{n-1}}{\sqrt2},$$
as required.''',['Counting','Recurrences','Graph walks','Parity'])
save()
